  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThrough Tonight: \n\nDry weather and seasonably warm temperatures will\npersist. There remains efficient afternoon mixing/low humidity \ninto critical levels (25 to 35 percent range) for continued \nElevated fire danger through early evening. High pressure remains\nin control and afternoon highs today are topping out in the upper\n80s F to lower 90s F before dropping into the lower 60s F\ntonight. /DC/86/\n\n\nFriday through midweek: \n\nA Fall, dry cold front will move across \nthe area tomorrow. An upper low currently over the Rockies will \nphase with deepening trough axis/upper jet diving into central \nCanada and southeast into the northern Plains to Mid MS Valley. \nThis will help an amplified meridional and positively tilted \ntrough. Stout, dry cold front will drive dewpoints falling into \nthe mid to upper 30s (36-45 F). Cannot rule out a rogue shower \nbut overall majority areas will remain dry. Cool down remains \nlikely this weekend. Highs will be seasonably warm Friday (72-78 F\nin the northwestern half of the area, 79-91 F in the southeastern\nhalf), before falling dramatically overnight with most locations \nfalling into the 40s F north of highway 84 and to near 50 F along\nand south of that corridor. With continued drought stressed \nvegetation, this strong gusty front will likely increase fire \ndanger concerns. The cold front will move into the Delta around \ndaybreak Friday, Interstate 20 around midday and out of the area \nby early evening. 1030-1040 mb surface high building will build \neast out of the lee side of the Rockies through the Mid West, \nleading to 6-8 mb pressure gradient and resultant gusty gradient \nwind of 20-25 mph sustained and wind gusts reaching 30-35 mph at \ntimes. Elevated Fire Weather conditions will persist through the \nweekend, peeking into Significant ranges Friday when a Red Flag \nwarning will be in effect. A Fire Weather Watch was also issued \nfor Saturday as gusts and low humidity will remain. As the trough\ndigs across the Eastern Seaboard and cuts off the Atlantic \nseaboard, ridge over the western Gulf will amplify, with the axis \nover the ArkLaTex. Seasonable, cooler fall temperatures will \nbuild areawide into Saturday and Sunday (highs lower 70s F \nSaturday and through the 70s F Sunday. Lows dip into middle 40s F\nnorth of Interstate 20 and upper 40s F to the south). Quiet and \ndry weather will persist next week, with an Omega block pattern \ndeveloping (trough over the Baja California and southwest US and \nanother just off the Carolina coast). Low level return flow and \ndeep dry moisture profiles will lead to moderating temperatures \n(low 80s F early week to mid to upper 80s F by late week) and lows\nbecoming more seasonable. With continued drought, stressed \nvegetation and afternoon humidity nearing critical levels by mid \nweek (25 to 35 percent), fire danger will likely persist.\nAdditional fire weather graphics will likely be necessary as we\napproach next week. /DC/86/",
